const quickAnswer = `The ideal TV viewing distance depends on screen size and resolution. For a 4K TV, the sweet spot is 1 to 1.5 times the screen diagonal. For a 65\" TV: sit 5.5–8 feet away. For a 75\": 6.5–9.5 feet. For an 85\": 7–11 feet. For a 98\": 8–12 feet. For a 1080p TV, the recommended range is wider — 1.5 to 2.5 times the diagonal — because you'll see pixelation sitting closer. The old "10 feet for every 30 inches" rule was written for 1080p; 4K lets you sit significantly closer without seeing pixels. At the correct distance, a 4K image is indistinguishable from film. The formula: multiply your screen diagonal in inches by 1.0 to 1.5, then convert to feet (divide by 12). That gives your ideal range. For most San Antonio living rooms with 10–14 foot seating distances, a 75–85\" 4K TV is the correct size.`;

const fullAnswer = `<h2>The Formula</h2>
<p><strong>For 4K TVs:</strong> Ideal distance (inches) = screen diagonal × 1.0 to 1.5<br>
<strong>For 1080p TVs:</strong> Ideal distance (inches) = screen diagonal × 1.5 to 2.5</p>
<p>To convert to feet: divide by 12. Example: 75\" TV × 1.5 = 112.5 inches ÷ 12 = 9.4 feet maximum comfortable distance.</p>

<h2>Quick Reference Table — 4K TVs</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Screen Size</th><th>Minimum Distance</th><th>Ideal Distance</th><th>Maximum Distance</th></tr>
  <tr><td>55\"</td><td>4.5 ft</td><td>5.5–6.5 ft</td><td>7 ft</td></tr>
  <tr><td>65\"</td><td>5.5 ft</td><td>6.5–8 ft</td><td>8.5 ft</td></tr>
  <tr><td>75\"</td><td>6.5 ft</td><td>7.5–9.5 ft</td><td>10 ft</td></tr>
  <tr><td>85\"</td><td>7 ft</td><td>8.5–11 ft</td><td>12 ft</td></tr>
  <tr><td>98\"</td><td>8 ft</td><td>10–12 ft</td><td>13.5 ft</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Why 4K Changes the Equation</h2>
<p>4K has four times the pixel density of 1080p. At the same screen size, you can sit twice as close before your eye resolves individual pixels. The practical result: a 4K 75\" TV in a 10-foot living room looks like a cinema screen, not a pixelated monitor. The old rules about sitting far back were written for 1080p and do not apply.</p>

<h2>What If My Room Is Smaller Than the Ideal Distance?</h2>
<p>If you are forced to sit closer than the minimum, go down in screen size rather than sit uncomfortably close. A 65\" at 6 feet looks better than an 85\" at 6 feet. However, in a dedicated theater where seating is fixed, a projector is often the right answer — it lets you set the screen size to exactly match the room's geometry.</p>

<h2>Viewing Angle: The Horizontal Factor</h2>
<p>The SMPTE standard for theater viewing recommends a 30° horizontal viewing angle as the minimum for immersion, and up to 40° for a cinematic feel. At 10 feet from a 75\" screen, your horizontal angle is approximately 36° — right in the sweet spot. THX recommends a 36° minimum. If you are sitting much wider than 30° off-center, sound and picture quality both degrade — that is when a projector or multiple displays should be considered.</p>

<h2>Dedicated Theater vs. Living Room</h2>
<p>In a dedicated theater with a fixed seating platform, we calculate the throw distance and seating rake before finalizing the screen size. This is the correct order: fix the room, fix the seating distance, then specify the screen. In a living room where seating can move, match the TV to the most common seating position — usually the primary sofa.</p>`;

const proTip = `The single most common mistake we see in living rooms: people buy the largest TV that physically fits the wall and then sit too far back. A 75\" 4K TV watched from 14 feet looks like a small picture. The right answer is almost always a bigger TV than people expect — and sitting closer than they think. We routinely recommend 85\"–98\" for living rooms with 10–14 foot seating distances.`;

const kzAngle = `San Antonio custom homes often have great rooms with 18–22 foot ceilings and open floor plans that push seating 14–16 feet from the wall. At those distances, an 85\" TV looks small. We regularly spec 98\" and larger displays — or projector-and-screen combinations — for Hill Country homes and Stone Oak estates where the room dimensions demand it.`;
